{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:\nThe component is an entirely inaccessible pseudo-widget built exclusively with unsemantic `<div>` elements and mouse-only click handlers. It completely bars keyboard-only and screen reader users from accessing site navigation, lacks any programmatic state communication (`aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`), and implements zero focus management or landmark structure.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore examining the code in detail, mega menu and dropdown components typically fail in the following ways:\n1. **Mouse-only triggers**: Using `div`/`span` with `onClick` instead of native `<button>` or `<a>` elements, blocking Tab focus. *(Confirmed: lines 10\u201314)*\n2. **Missing state communication**: Lack of `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` to announce open/collapsed states to assistive technology. *(Confirmed: lines 13\u201316)*\n3. **No keyboard dismiss / focus restoration**: Absence of `Escape` key handling to close menus and restore focus to triggers. *(Confirmed: lines 10\u201326)*\n4. **Missing list/landmark semantics**: Nesting in arbitrary `div` containers rather than `<nav>`, `<ul>`, and `<li>`. *(Confirmed: lines 7\u20138, 17)*\n5. **Inaccessible submenu items**: Child items rendered as plain text/divs without keyboard-focusable links or buttons. *(Confirmed: lines 19\u201323)*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Interactive triggers are non-focusable `<div>` elements without keyboard support\n- **Location**: `BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:10-14`\n- **Impacted Group**: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users, Switch access users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: `<div className=\"menu-item\" onClick={...}>` is neither natively focusable nor exposed as an interactive role to accessibility APIs. Keyboard users pressing `Tab` skip past the navigation entirely. Screen reader users encounter a generic text container with no indication that it is clickable or acts as a menu trigger.\n- **Fix**: Replace the outer interactive wrapper with a native `<button type=\"button\">` (or an anchor paired with a disclosure button per the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Navigation Menu pattern).\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: megamenu-non-semantic-div-trigger\nfingerprint: a11e8901b44c21ef\nsource: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:10-14\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / 508 Chapter 5 (maps to WCAG 2.0 A/AA)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard_only=HIGH, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:10-14: <div className=\"menu-item\" onClick={() => setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)}>\nreproduction_steps: 1. Render BuggyMegaMenu with items. 2. Press Tab key to navigate into the menu. 3. Observe that focus bypasses all menu items completely.\nexpected_behavior: Menu triggers must be in the tab order, announce their role as buttons/disclosures, and activate on Space and Enter.\nactual_behavior: Triggers are non-focusable <div> elements ignored by keyboard navigation and announced as static text by screen readers.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. Submenu items are static `<div>` elements lacking interaction semantics\n- **Location**: `BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:20-22`\n- **Impacted Group**: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: `<div className=\"submenu-item\">{child}</div>` renders submenu targets as unnavigable static text. Even if a mouse user clicks the top-level trigger, a keyboard user cannot Tab or arrow into the submenu to select any child destination.\n- **Fix**: Submenu items must contain semantic interactive targets, typically `<a href=\"...\">` for site navigation links, housed inside list items `<li>`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: megamenu-static-submenu-items\nfingerprint: c58921df843a02bb\nsource: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:20-22\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / 508 Chapter 5 (maps to WCAG 2.0 A/AA)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard_only=HIGH, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=LOW\nevidence: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:20-22: <div key={cidx} className=\"submenu-item\">{child}</div>\nreproduction_steps: 1. Click menu item to reveal submenu. 2. Attempt to Tab or navigate into submenu children via keyboard.\nexpected_behavior: Submenu items should contain focusable, actionable links (<a>) or buttons (<button>).\nactual_behavior: Children are plain <div> containers that cannot receive focus or be activated via keyboard.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Expansion state is not communicated to assistive technology\n- **Location**: `BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:13-17`\n- **Impacted Group**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: The open/closed state is managed solely via React state (`openMenu === idx`) controlling DOM presence. There is no `aria-expanded=\"true|false\"` attribute on the trigger and no `aria-controls` referencing the submenu container. Screen reader users receive no feedback that activating the item reveals new content or what section was revealed.\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={openMenu === idx}` and `aria-controls={`submenu-${idx}`}` to the trigger button, and assign `id={`submenu-${idx}`}` to the submenu panel.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: megamenu-missing-aria-expanded\nfingerprint: d74810ac90b1e421\nsource: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:13-17\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / 508 Chapter 5\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard_only=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:13-17: onClick={() => setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)} with no aria-expanded or aria-controls attributes.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Inspect trigger element with accessibility tree viewer. 2. Toggle the menu. 3. Observe no state change in accessibility tree.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader should announce \"expanded\" or \"collapsed\" when state toggles.\nactual_behavior: State change is purely visual; accessibility tree reflects no state property.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. Complete absence of focus management, dismissal, and keyboard trapping/escape handling\n- **Location**: `BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:4, 10-26`\n- **Impacted Group**: Keyboard-only users, Cognitive accessibility users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap (Level A), WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Navigation Menus\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: There is no mechanism to close an open submenu via the `Escape` key, no focus restoration to the parent trigger when closed, and no outside-click or blur listener. If a user opens a submenu, they cannot dismiss it without locating and re-clicking the exact same trigger.\n- **Fix**: Implement an `onKeyDown` listener that collapses the active submenu and returns focus to the parent trigger on `Escape`. Close open menus when focus moves completely outside the component (`onBlur`).\n\n#### 3. Complete lack of semantic landmark and list structure\n- **Location**: `BuggyMegaMenu.jsx:7-9, 17-19`\n- **Impacted Group**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Navigation menus must be enclosed in `<nav aria-label=\"...\">` landmark containers, with items grouped in `<ul>` and `<li>` elements. Currently, the tree is a generic tag soup of `div`s (`megamenu-container`, `megamenu-items`, `submenu`). Screen reader users cannot use landmark navigation shortcuts (e.g., `M` or `D` keys) to locate the main navigation or determine how many navigation items exist.\n- **Fix**: Wrap the container in `<nav aria-label=\"Main Navigation\">`, replace item wrappers with `<ul>` and `<li>`.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings & Enhancements\n\n#### Minor Findings\n- **Missing accessible labeling for disclosure state indicators**: (Line 15) If chevron icons or symbols are added visually to show dropdown state, ensure they are hidden with `aria-hidden=\"true\"` to prevent screen readers from announcing \"plus\", \"down arrow\", etc., alongside programmatic state.\n\n#### Enhancements\n- **Hover vs. Click intent**: If hover interaction is ever introduced in CSS, ensure mobile touch and keyboard focus explicitly mirror click behavior, and respect WCAG 1.4.13 (Content on Hover or Focus: dismissible, hoverable, persistent).\n- **APG pattern alignment**: Use the **WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Navigation Menu** pattern rather than desktop `role=\"menubar\"`. Web site navigation menus should maintain standard `Tab` flow between top-level items and links rather than trapping arrow keys like desktop application menus.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gaps & Edge Cases)\n\n1. **Navigation Landmark**: Missing `<nav>` element with an accessible name (`aria-label=\"Main\"`).\n2. **List Semantics**: Missing `<ul>` and `<li>` markup to provide item counts and hierarchical context (\"item 1 of 5\").\n3. **Trigger Disclosures**: Missing `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`.\n4. **Escape Key Handling**: No keydown listener to collapse menus on `Escape`.\n5. **Focus Restoration**: No mechanism to return focus to the trigger button when an open submenu is dismissed.\n6. **Outside Interaction Dismissal**: No pointer/focus listener to close open menus when user clicks or tabs outside the menu container.\n7. **Interactive Submenu Anchors**: Missing `<a href=\"...\">` links inside child items.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Review\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Disastrous. Announces as an undifferentiated block of unlabelled text divs. User cannot jump to it via landmarks, has no idea it is a menu, cannot tell what items have submenus, and cannot perceive whether submenus are open.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Completely locked out. Cannot tab into the menu, cannot activate items with Space or Enter, cannot navigate submenus, and cannot dismiss submenus with Escape.\n- **Low vision user**: No focus visible rings exist because elements cannot be focused. Lack of proper structure hinders screen magnifier tracking.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: High cognitive friction. No visual cues (e.g., chevrons) indicating expandable content, and no predictable way to dismiss opened content.\n\n#### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Complete lack of ARIA roles, states (`aria-expanded`), and semantic HTML (`<nav>`, `<ul>`, `<li>`). |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Zero focusable elements; mouse-only `onClick` on non-focusable `<div>`. |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | No focus indicator states defined; risk of content clipping/obscuration without proper container sizing. |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Missing visual and programmatic affordances for expandable sections; no Escape dismissal. |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations or motion triggers present. |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No media or audio content present. |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | Focus outlines completely absent since elements are non-interactive divs. |\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REJECT**\n\nThis component violates fundamental Level A criteria of WCAG 2.2:\n- **WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard)**: Non-operable via keyboard.\n- **WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)**: Divs used instead of semantic buttons/links; state completely missing.\n- **WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships)**: Semantic structure replaced by unsemantic `div` hierarchy.\n\nTo upgrade this component to **ACCEPT**, the architecture must be refactored to use native semantic HTML (`<nav>`, `<ul>`, `<li>`, `<button>`, `<a>`), proper ARIA disclosure attributes (`aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`), and keyboard interaction handling (`Escape` handling and focus management).\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleMegaMenu = ({ items = [], label = \"Main Navigation\" }) => {\n  const [openMenu, setOpenMenu] = useState(null);\n  const menuRef = useRef(null);\n  const triggerRefs = useRef([]);\n\n  // Close menu on click outside or focus loss\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const handleKeyDown = (event) => {\n      if (event.key === 'Escape' && openMenu !== null) {\n        const triggerToFocus = triggerRefs.current[openMenu];\n        setOpenMenu(null);\n        if (triggerToFocus) triggerToFocus.focus();\n      }\n    };\n\n    const handleClickOutside = (event) => {\n      if (menuRef.current && !menuRef.current.contains(event.target)) {\n        setOpenMenu(null);\n      }\n    };\n\n    document.addEventListener('keydown', handleKeyDown);\n    document.addEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);\n    return () => {\n      document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleKeyDown);\n      document.removeEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);\n    };\n  }, [openMenu]);\n\n  return (\n    <nav className=\"megamenu-container\" aria-label={label} ref={menuRef}>\n      <ul className=\"megamenu-items\" role=\"list\">\n        {items.map((item, idx) => {\n          const isOpen = openMenu === idx;\n          const submenuId = `submenu-${idx}`;\n\n          return (\n            <li key={idx} className=\"menu-item\">\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                ref={(el) => (triggerRefs.current[idx] = el)}\n                aria-expanded={isOpen}\n                aria-controls={submenuId}\n                onClick={() => setOpenMenu(isOpen ? null : idx)}\n                className=\"menu-trigger-button\"\n              >\n                {item.label}\n                <span aria-hidden=\"true\" className=\"disclosure-indicator\">\n                  {isOpen ? ' \u25b2' : ' \u25bc'}\n                </span>\n              </button>\n\n              {isOpen && (\n                <div id={submenuId} className=\"submenu\">\n                  <ul className=\"submenu-list\" role=\"list\">\n                    {item.children &&\n                      item.children.map((child, cidx) => (\n                        <li key={cidx} className=\"submenu-item\">\n                          {/* Ensure child renders a valid interactive link or button */}\n                          {typeof child === 'string' ? (\n                            <a href={`#${child.toLowerCase().replace(/\\s+/g, '-')}`}>{child}</a>\n                          ) : (\n                            child\n                          )}\n                        </li>\n                      ))}\n                  </ul>\n                </div>\n              )}\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleMegaMenu;\n```\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. *Child item schema*: What data structure does `item.children` provide? If items are links, they must render as `<a href=\"...\">`; if they are action triggers, they should render as `<button>`.\n2. *Arrow key navigation requirement*: Does the design system require arrow key navigation (Roving Tabindex / Menu APG), or standard Tab-based disclosure navigation? (For standard web site menus, the APG Disclosure Navigation pattern with standard Tab navigation is strongly recommended).\n",
